  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nA cold front and upper trough will deliver a combination of lower\ntemperatures, increasing west to northwest winds, and continued \nshowers and thunderstorms for Sunday. SPC maintains a Marginal \nRisk for the east where severe wind gusts are a concern. These \nlocations will see the warmest daytime temperatures and more \nunstable conditions. Overall, HREF shows timing as early as 18Z \nfor the wind threat emerging, with activity evolving east by \nevening hours. A few of the storms may produce locally heavy \nrainfall as well.\n\nAs west winds ramp up on Sunday, a Lake Wind Advisory for Fort\nPeck Lake remains in effect until 6 PM MDT. Windy conditions will\nrelax during the evening but ramp back up on Monday. That said,\nwill defer headline decisions until later as trends have shifted\nwinds to be more focused on the east side of the lake.\n\nUpper ridging will then guide warmer and drier conditions to NE\nMontana for Tuesday. The ridge axis looks to slide east late in\nthe week with diffluent southwest upper flow becoming established\nover the Northern High Plains. In short, the next round of\nthunderstorms may get underway as early as Wednesday evening.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM was given a modest blend with NBM90 for an upward nudge given\nthe cold air advection. Pops were adjusted for near term\ncollaboration and consistency. Otherwise there were no major\ndeviations with model consensus lending to above average\nconfidence this forecast cycle for cooler temperatures with\nthunderstorm chances on Sunday, followed by quiet weather on\nTuesday. Confidence is also increasing on more active weather in\nthe form of thunderstorms by midweek as the ridge translates \ndownstream, supported by dynamic lift and increased instability.",
